// CS111 lab policy: Sublime must NOT run Python files. Ctrl+B just opens a
// Terminus tab at the home folder; students compile and run by hand from there.
//
// WHY: when Ctrl+B runs the file for them, students never notice WHERE the file
// lives — and then submit the wrong file to the grader. Making them pwd/ls/cd to
// their own file is the point of the exercise, not an obstacle to it.
//
// HOW THIS WORKS: Sublime resolves package resources by path, and a loose file on
// disk WINS over the same path inside a .sublime-package zip. This file therefore
// shadows the "Python.sublime-build" that ships inside Sublime's own
// Python.sublime-package, whose build is ["python3", "-u", "$file"]. That built-in
// never loads, so Ctrl+B, F7, Ctrl+Shift+B and Tools > Build all reach THIS file
// instead. Because the BUILD is neutered rather than the keys, no keymap changes
// are needed and the Tools menu is covered for free.
//
// The override is per-FILE, not per-package: Python syntax highlighting,
// completions and the rest of Python.sublime-package are unaffected.
//
// The built-in "Syntax Check" variant (python3 -m py_compile) is deliberately not
// redefined here, so it disappears along with the run build.
//
// TERMINUS, not an external terminal emulator: "target": "terminus_open" hands the
// build off to the Terminus package (already installed/required lab-wide) instead
// of spawning gnome-terminal/xterm/etc. Same "like Alt+`" interactive tab, just
// triggered from Ctrl+B/Ctrl+Shift+B/F7/Tools > Build too.
//
// DELIBERATE: opens at HOME, NOT at the file's directory, and says NOTHING — no
// path, no filename, no instructions. Pre-navigating or hinting would defeat the
// point. Neither $file_path nor $file_name is referenced anywhere.
//
// shell_cmd does `cd ~` then `exec bash -l` — exec REPLACES the shell_cmd
// process with an interactive login shell, so the Terminus tab doesn't just run
// one command and go dead; it stays open exactly like a normal terminal. cd is
// done in shell_cmd (not via Terminus's own "cwd" option) so behavior doesn't
// depend on whether Terminus expands "~" itself.
//
// Hardcoded "bash", NOT "$SHELL" or "${SHELL:-bash}": Sublime expands
// "${var:default}" itself (snippet-style variable substitution) in shell_cmd
// BEFORE the shell ever sees the string. SHELL isn't a Sublime build variable,
// so "${SHELL:-bash}" silently becomes the literal text "-bash" — which then
// fails as `exec "-bash" -l` (invalid option). Escaping the $ doesn't help
// either: Terminus performs its OWN second expansion pass downstream and
// mangles it again. Verified live in Sublime 4200 + Terminus 0.3.30. Terminus
// already only ever launches bash on Linux, so hardcoding loses nothing.
//
// Each Ctrl+B opens a NEW Terminus tab; repeated presses open several.
//
// The key is "shell_cmd", NOT "shell_command": ExecCommand.run() in Sublime's own
// Default/exec.py takes shell_cmd, and an unknown key raises
//   __init__() got an unexpected keyword argument ...
// which surfaces to the student as a traceback in the build panel. Verified on
// Sublime 4192. Terminus's terminus_open reuses the same "shell_cmd" key.
//
// To re-enable the normal build, delete this file. Nothing else references it.
{
	"selector": "source.python",
	"target": "terminus_open",
	"shell_cmd": "cd ~ 2>/dev/null; exec bash -l",
	"auto_close": false,
	"focus": true,
	"title": "Build"
}
